MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m09",
  "summary": "Understand vehicle electrical circuits and use Ohm's law, a multimeter and voltage-drop testing to find open circuits, shorts and high-resistance faults, and repair wiring correctly.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain current, voltage, resistance and power, and use Ohm's law and the power formula.","Describe series, parallel and series-parallel circuits and the effects of opens, shorts and high resistance.","Name common circuit components such as fuses, relays, diodes and switches, and explain what each does.","Use a digital multimeter to measure voltage, resistance, continuity and current safely.","Carry out a voltage-drop test on the power and earth sides of a circuit and interpret the result.","Find an electrical fault with a logical method and repair a wire so that it is safe and waterproof."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Ohm's law: V = I × R. Power: P = V × I.","In series circuits the current is the same everywhere. In parallel circuits the voltage is the same across each branch.","Vehicles use the metal body and chassis as the return path (earth/ground) for most circuits.","Never replace a fuse with a higher rating. A fuse protects the wire, not the load.","A relay uses a small current in its coil to switch a large current through its contacts.","Measure voltage in parallel with the load. Measure current in series. Measure resistance only with power off.","Voltage drop shows high resistance in a live circuit: measure across the section while it is working.","Half-split method: test in the middle of the circuit, then keep halving the faulty half.","A good wire repair is crimped or soldered, sealed with adhesive heat-shrink and supported so it does not flex."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m09-l1", "title": "Electricity in vehicles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A modern vehicle has many electrical circuits, from the starter to the lights, sensors and computers. To work on them, you need a clear understanding of three basic quantities and how they relate."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"ohm-triangle"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Voltage, current and resistance"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Voltage (V)** is the electrical pressure that pushes current round a circuit. It is measured in volts.","**Current (I)** is the flow of electrical charge. It is measured in amperes (amps, A).","**Resistance (R)** is the opposition to current flow. It is measured in ohms (Ω)."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Think of water in a pipe. Voltage is the pressure, current is the flow of water and resistance is a narrow section that restricts the flow."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Prefix","Symbol","Meaning","Example"],"rows":[["kilo","k","1,000","4.7 kΩ = 4,700 Ω"],["mega","M","1,000,000","10 MΩ = 10,000,000 Ω (the input resistance of a meter)"],["milli","m","one thousandth","250 mA = 0.25 A"],["micro","µ","one millionth","100 µA = 0.0001 A"]],"caption":"Always convert to volts, amps and ohms before you use a formula."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Materials are conductors, insulators or semiconductors. **Conductors**, such as copper and aluminium, let current flow easily, and are used for wires. **Insulators**, such as plastic and rubber, block current, and cover the wires. **Semiconductors**, such as silicon, conduct under certain conditions. They are used to make diodes, transistors and the chips in every control unit. Vehicle circuits use **direct current** (DC), which flows in one direction. The alternator makes **alternating current** (AC) inside, which diodes convert to DC before it reaches the battery."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ohm's law"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"V = I × R","caption":"Voltage (volts) = current (amps) × resistance (ohms). Rearranged: I = V ÷ R and R = V ÷ I."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Current in a lamp circuit","steps":["A lamp has a resistance of 6 Ω and is connected to 12 V.","I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A.","If the resistance doubles to 12 Ω because of a corroded connector, the current falls to 12 ÷ 12 = 1 A and the lamp is dim."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"P = V × I","caption":"Power (watts) = voltage (volts) × current (amps)."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A 55 W headlamp bulb on a 12 V system draws 55 ÷ 12 = about 4.6 A. Knowing the current helps you to choose the right fuse and wire size."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The vehicle system"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most cars and light vehicles use a 12 V battery system. Heavy vehicles often use 24 V, and some mild hybrids add a 48 V system. The vehicle body and chassis are connected to the battery negative and used as the return path, called the **earth (ground)**. This saves wire, but it means that a poor earth connection can cause faults anywhere on the vehicle. A circuit has a power side (between the battery positive and the load) and an earth side (between the load and the battery negative)."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Conventional current and electron flow"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Wiring diagrams and manuals show current flowing from the positive terminal round the circuit to the negative, called **conventional current**. In a wire, it is actually electrons that move, and they flow the other way. This does not matter for diagnosis: use the conventional direction when you read diagrams, and think of current flowing from the battery positive, through the fuse, switch and load, and back to the negative through the earth."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A 12 V battery can still deliver hundreds of amps into a short circuit. A spanner across the terminals can weld itself in place and cause burns or a fire. Remove rings and watches before working near the battery."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Write the formula triangle on your toolbox lid, with V on top and I and R at the bottom. Cover the quantity you want, and the other two show how to calculate it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l2", "title": "Circuit types and faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Vehicle circuits are built from a few basic patterns. When you can recognise these patterns, you can predict what the voltage and current should be, and find faults faster."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"series-parallel"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Series circuits"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **series circuit**, the components are connected one after another, so the same current flows through all of them. The total resistance is the sum of the individual resistances. The voltage divides between the components in proportion to their resistance. A break anywhere stops the current in the whole circuit."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parallel circuits"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **parallel circuit**, each component is on its own branch connected across the same two points, so each receives the full supply voltage. The currents in the branches add up. If one branch fails, the others still work. Lamps and other loads in a vehicle are usually wired in parallel."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Series-parallel circuits"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Real circuits often mix series and parallel parts. To work out the whole, reduce each parallel group to one equivalent resistance, then add the series parts. A common example is a pair of lamps in parallel, fed through a switch and a fuse in series. The current through the fuse and switch is the total for both lamps, and each lamp carries its own share."},
      {"t":"example","title":"A series-parallel circuit","steps":["A 12 V supply feeds a switch with a resistance of 0.1 Ω, then two lamps in parallel (3 Ω each), then a 0.1 Ω earth path.","The two lamps in parallel make 1.5 Ω. Add the series parts: 0.1 + 1.5 + 0.1 = 1.7 Ω.","Current = 12 ÷ 1.7 = 7.06 A.","The lamps get 7.06 × 1.5 = 10.6 V, not the full 12 V, because 1.4 V is lost in the switch and earth. If the switch resistance rose to 0.5 Ω, the lamps would drop to about 9.8 V. The lamps then glow dimmer, and the whole circuit shows how small resistances in series matter."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Voltage dividers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Two resistors in series across a supply form a **voltage divider**. The voltage at the point between them is a fraction of the supply. Many sensors work this way, such as a temperature sensor in series with a fixed resistor, where the ECU measures the voltage between them."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"basic-vehicle-circuit"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Faults in circuits"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","What it is","Typical effect"],"rows":[["Open circuit","A break in the path, such as a broken wire or a blown bulb","The load does not work. No current flows"],["Short to earth","Power-side wire touches the body or an earth wire","Current rises, fuse blows, or the load is bypassed"],["Short to power","Two circuits touch, or a wire touches the live supply","A load that works when it should not, or a blown fuse"],["High resistance","Corroded, loose or damaged connection in the path","Dim lights, slow motors, low voltage at the load"]],"caption":"Common circuit faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wire size and voltage drop"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Every wire has some resistance. A long or thin wire has more. When current flows, some voltage is lost along the wire. This is called **voltage drop**. If the wire is too thin for the load, the load gets too little voltage and the wire heats up. Always use a wire of the correct size, rated for the current, and keep the length as short as practical."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Two batteries: series and parallel"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Heavy vehicles often have two 12 V batteries. Connected in **series**, positive of one to negative of the other, they give 24 V with the same capacity. Connected in **parallel**, positive to positive and negative to negative, they stay at 12 V but give twice the capacity and twice the starting current. This is the idea behind jump leads, which connect a good battery in parallel with a flat one. Connect the leads in the right order, and never connect batteries of different voltages."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Kirchhoff's current rule in plain words"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The current that flows into any junction in a circuit equals the current that flows out of it. It means that in a parallel circuit, the supply current is the sum of the branch currents. If the supply current is 9 A and one branch carries 4 A, the others together carry 5 A. When the numbers do not add up, something is not as you thought, such as a leak to earth or a wrong wire."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A high-resistance fault does not stop a circuit working. It only makes it work poorly, which is why it is harder to find than an open circuit."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A wire that is too thin for the current can heat up and set fire to the insulation. Never fit a thin wire to a high-current load."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l3", "title": "Circuit calculations, wire sizes and fuses", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Knowing the numbers behind a circuit helps you predict what a good circuit should do and to see what is wrong with a faulty one. This lesson covers series and parallel calculations, voltage dividers, wire sizing, voltage drop in cables and choosing a fuse. The calculations are simple, and every step uses Ohm's law."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"fuse-colours"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Series circuits"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"R total = R1 + R2 + R3","caption":"In a series circuit the same current flows through every part, and the voltages add up to the supply voltage."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Two resistors in series","steps":["A 12 V supply drives a 4 Ω and a 2 Ω resistor in series.","Total resistance = 4 + 2 = 6 Ω.","Current = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A, the same in both.","Voltage across the 4 Ω resistor = 2 × 4 = 8 V, and across the 2 Ω resistor = 2 × 2 = 4 V. Together they make 12 V."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parallel circuits"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"1 ÷ R total = 1 ÷ R1 + 1 ÷ R2 + 1 ÷ R3","caption":"In a parallel circuit every branch has the full supply voltage, and the branch currents add up. The total resistance is always less than the smallest branch."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Two headlamps in parallel","steps":["Each lamp has a resistance of 3 Ω on a 12 V supply.","Current in each lamp = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 A. Total current = 4 + 4 = 8 A.","Total resistance = 12 ÷ 8 = 1.5 Ω. For two equal resistors in parallel the total is half of one, so 3 ÷ 2 = 1.5 Ω.","If one lamp blows, the other still has 12 V and 4 A and remains lit."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Voltage dividers"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"V out = V in × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2)","caption":"R2 is the resistor the output voltage is measured across."},
      {"t":"example","title":"A temperature sensor circuit","steps":["The ECU supplies 5 V through a fixed 1 kΩ resistor to a coolant sensor, which is connected to earth. The voltage is read at the junction.","When the sensor is cold, its resistance is 2.5 kΩ, so V out = 5 × 2,500 ÷ (1,000 + 2,500) = 3.57 V.","When it is hot, its resistance is 240 Ω, so V out = 5 × 240 ÷ (1,000 + 240) = 0.97 V.","The voltage falls as the engine warms, and the ECU reads it as a temperature."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power and energy"},
      {"t":"example","title":"A headlamp circuit and the battery","steps":["A 55 W headlamp on 12 V draws 55 ÷ 12 = 4.6 A. Two lamps draw 9.2 A.","A battery of 60 amp-hours can supply 60 A for one hour, or 6 A for ten hours, in theory.","At 9.2 A it would last about 60 ÷ 9.2 = 6.5 hours, if fully charged. In practice, a battery should not be run below about 50% charge, so the usable time is about 3 hours."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Choosing a wire size"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A wire must carry its current without overheating or dropping too much voltage. Wire is sized by the cross-section of the copper, in square millimetres. These are typical safe currents for wire in a vehicle loom. Always check the manufacturer's values for the wire and the length."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Cross-section","Typical safe current","Typical use"],"rows":[["0.5 mm²","About 5 A","Sensors, small lamps"],["1.0 mm²","About 10 A","Side lights, relays"],["1.5 mm²","About 15 A","Headlamps, horn"],["2.5 mm²","About 20 to 25 A","Fans, wiper motors, fuel pump"],["4 mm²","About 30 to 35 A","Heated rear window, big fans"],["10 mm²","About 60 to 70 A","Alternator output"],["25 mm² and above","About 120 A and more","Starter feed, main battery cables"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Voltage drop in a cable"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"R = 0.0172 × L ÷ A","caption":"R in ohms, L is the total length in metres out and back, A is the copper area in mm². The 0.0172 is the resistivity of copper."},
      {"t":"example","title":"A long feed to a rear lamp","steps":["A rear lamp takes 5 A. The wire is 1.5 mm² and 5 m long each way, so the total length out and back is 10 m.","R = 0.0172 × 10 ÷ 1.5 = 0.115 Ω.","Voltage drop = 5 × 0.115 = 0.57 V. This is just over the 0.5 V limit, so a 2.5 mm² wire would be better: R = 0.0172 × 10 ÷ 2.5 = 0.069 Ω and drop = 0.34 V."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Choosing a fuse"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Pick a fuse that is above the normal operating current, usually by 25 to 50%, to avoid nuisance blowing, and well below what the wire can carry. For a 5 A load on a 1.0 mm² wire (safe up to 10 A) the best fuse is 7.5 A or 10 A, not 20 A. Motor loads draw a large current for a moment when they start, so a motor fuse is chosen to allow for that. The fuse rating is a limit on the wire, not a statement about the load."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Before you add an accessory, work out its current, choose a wire to carry it and a fuse to protect that wire, and fit the fuse close to the power source."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never fit a fuse of a higher rating to stop it blowing. The wire may overheat and start a fire before the fuse blows."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l4", "title": "Circuit components", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Vehicle circuits are built from a small number of component types. This lesson explains what each one does and what faults to expect."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Protection devices"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Fuse:** a thin strip of metal that melts and opens the circuit if the current is too high. It protects the wire. Always replace a fuse with one of the same rating. A bigger fuse lets the wire overheat before it blows.","**Fusible link:** a short length of special wire that works as a fuse in high-current supply lines.","**Circuit breaker:** a switch that opens when the current is too high, and can be reset, as in some power windows."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Blade fuse colour","Rating"],"rows":[["Tan","5 A"],["Brown","7.5 A"],["Red","10 A"],["Blue","15 A"],["Yellow","20 A"],["Clear or white","25 A"],["Green","30 A"]],"caption":"Standard colours for blade fuses. The rating is also printed on the top."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Large **maxi** fuses protect high-current feeds, and **fusible links** protect the main battery cable. The fuse is chosen to blow at a current a little above the normal load but below the current the wire can safely carry, so it protects the wire. Always fit the correct size and type. A fuse is not a part to be replaced with a bolt or a bit of foil."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Switches and relays"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **switch** opens and closes a circuit by hand. A **relay** is an electrically operated switch. A small current through its coil makes an electromagnet that pulls the contacts closed, so a large current can flow through them. Relays let a thin wire and a small dashboard switch control a heavy load such as headlamps or a fuel pump. The standard terminals are 85 and 86 for the coil and 30 and 87 for the switch contacts, with 87a for a normally closed contact."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"relay-operation"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other components"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Component","What it does","Common fault"],"rows":[["Resistor","Limits current or divides voltage","Open circuit, changed value"],["Diode","Allows current in one direction only","Open or short, fails to block"],["Capacitor","Stores a small amount of charge and smooths voltage","Leaks or opens"],["Solenoid","A coil that moves a plunger when energised","Burned coil, sticky plunger"],["Motor","Turns electrical energy into rotation","Worn brushes, open windings"],["Thermistor","Resistance varies with temperature","Wrong value, open or short"]],"caption":"Common components"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Connectors and wiring"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Connectors join wires and components. They can corrode, loosen or have bent pins, which cause high-resistance and intermittent faults. Wires are colour coded, and circuit symbols on diagrams show each component. Colours and symbols vary between makers, so always use the diagram for the vehicle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Transistors and electronic switching"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **transistor** is a tiny electronic switch with no moving parts. A small current at one terminal, the base or gate, allows a much larger current to flow between the other two. Control units use transistors, such as the ones in the injector and coil drivers, to switch loads on and off thousands of times per second. A transistor can be damaged by too much current, a voltage spike or static, and when it fails it usually stays on or off, so the load never switches or never switches off. You rarely repair a transistor on its own. A faulty driver means a new control unit or driver module."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When a relay is suspect, swap it with an identical relay from another circuit, such as the horn, and see if the fault moves. Replace it afterwards."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A fuse that blows again is telling you there is a fault. Do not just fit a bigger fuse. Find and repair the cause."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l5", "title": "Magnetism, relays, solenoids and motors", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A great deal of vehicle electrics depends on one idea: an electric current makes a magnetic field, and a magnetic field can make a current. This single idea explains relays, solenoids, starter motors, alternators, ignition coils and many sensors. Understanding it helps you test these parts and know what goes wrong."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electromagnetism"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A current in a wire makes a magnetic field around it. Winding the wire into a **coil** concentrates the field, and a core of soft iron strengthens it. The result is an **electromagnet**, which is strongest when the current is large and the number of turns is large. When the current stops, the field collapses. A relay, a solenoid and an injector are all electromagnets that move a part when they are switched on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Induction"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a magnetic field moves across a wire, or the field around a wire changes, a voltage is **induced** in the wire. This is **electromagnetic induction**. It is how an alternator makes electricity, and how an ignition coil makes a high voltage: when the primary current stops, the collapsing field induces thousands of volts in the secondary coil. Some sensors, such as an inductive crankshaft sensor, use the same effect, as the teeth of a wheel pass a magnet and coil."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Voltage spikes and suppression diodes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the current to a coil is switched off, the collapsing field induces a brief high voltage in the coil itself, often hundreds of volts, called a **voltage spike** or **back EMF**. In a relay or solenoid this spike can damage the transistor that switches it. Many relays and solenoids therefore have a **suppression diode** across the coil, to give the spike a safe path. A relay with a diode must be fitted the right way round, because the coil terminals have polarity. If you connect a test supply to such a relay the wrong way round, the diode conducts and may damage it or blow a fuse."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Relays"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Terminal","Job"],"rows":[["85","Coil, usually connected to earth or to the ECU"],["86","Coil, usually connected to the switched supply"],["30","Common contact, the power feed"],["87","Normally open contact, the output when the coil is energised"],["87a","Normally closed contact, used in a changeover relay"]],"caption":"Most vehicle relays are rated for 20 to 40 A. Terminal numbers are standard, but check the diagram."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Measure the coil resistance across 85 and 86. A typical value is 60 to 120 Ω. OL means an open coil, and a very low reading means a short.","Check for continuity across 30 and 87 with the relay at rest (none expected) and then with 12 V on the coil (continuity expected, with a click).","With the relay in its socket and the circuit on, measure the voltage across the contacts. A relay that switches a load should drop under 0.2 V. A large drop means burnt contacts.","In the vehicle, check for 12 V at the coil supply and the earth path, and at terminal 30. If these are good and the relay does not click, replace it."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Solenoids"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **solenoid** is a coil that pulls an iron plunger in when it is energised. It operates fuel injectors, door locks, starter engagement, valves and shift mechanisms. A solenoid may have a high resistance, a few ohms to a few hundred ohms. A coil that has lost its insulation, an open winding or a sticking plunger will fail the test. A starter solenoid has two windings, a heavy **pull-in** winding and a lighter **hold-in** winding, and carries heavy current in its main contacts."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electric motors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **DC motor** has a rotor with coils, a magnetic field from permanent magnets or field coils, and **brushes** that feed current to the rotor through a **commutator**. A current in the rotor coils, in the magnetic field, makes a force that turns the rotor. The faster it turns, the more voltage it induces in itself, which opposes the supply and limits the current. This is why a **stalled** motor draws a very high current. A wiper motor jammed against ice, for example, may draw many times its running current, so the fuse blows or the motor overheats."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Symptom"],"rows":[["Worn brushes or a dirty commutator","Slow, weak or intermittent motor, sparking"],["Open winding","Motor does not turn at certain positions"],["Seized bearings","Motor hums, draws high current and blows the fuse"],["High resistance in the feed or earth","Slow motor, hot connector"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A motor that runs slowly and draws too much current has a mechanical or electrical fault. A motor that runs slowly and draws too little current has a supply problem such as voltage drop."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check a motor by measuring its current with a clamp meter and comparing it with the specification. It tells you more than a voltage reading alone."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l6", "title": "Using a digital multimeter", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The digital multimeter (DMM) is your main electrical tool. This lesson shows how to use it correctly and avoid common mistakes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Voltage"},
      {"t":"p","text":"To measure DC voltage, set the meter to DC volts and connect it **in parallel** across the component or between two points. The red lead goes to the more positive point and the black lead goes to the less positive point or earth. Measure battery voltage first. A healthy battery reads about 12.6 V at rest, and the charging system gives about 13.8 to 14.7 V with the engine running. Check the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Resistance and continuity"},
      {"t":"p","text":"To measure resistance, the circuit must be **switched off** and the component disconnected, or the reading will be wrong and the meter can be damaged. Set the meter to ohms, touch the leads together to check it reads near zero, then measure. The continuity setting beeps when the path is complete. An open circuit reads OL (over limit)."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Current"},
      {"t":"p","text":"To measure current, the meter must be connected **in series**, so the current flows through it. Use the correct current socket and range, with the lead connected to the amps socket. Current over the meter's fuse rating will blow the fuse. For large currents, use a **clamp meter**, which measures the magnetic field round a wire without breaking the circuit."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diode test and other functions"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The diode test setting checks diodes, such as those in an alternator. A good diode shows a voltage drop of about 0.5 to 0.7 V in one direction and OL in the other. Many meters also read frequency, duty cycle and temperature."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Test lamps and back-probing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A conventional **test lamp** draws a lot of current and can damage electronic circuits and sensors. Use a DMM on electronic circuits. When you test a connector that is plugged in, **back-probe** it from the rear with a fine probe, without damaging the pins or the seals. Do not push probes into the front of the terminals."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Common error","Result"],"rows":[["Measuring resistance with the power on","Wrong reading, possible meter damage"],["Measuring current with the meter in parallel","Blown meter fuse or a short circuit"],["Leads in the wrong sockets","Wrong reading or blown fuse"],["Poor contact with a probe","Unsteady or wrong readings"]],"caption":"Common meter errors"},
      {"t":"h","text":"What to know about your meter"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Input resistance:** a good digital meter has an input resistance of 10 MΩ on its voltage ranges, so it takes almost no current from the circuit. That makes it safe on electronic circuits. An old analogue meter may draw enough to upset them.","**Ghost voltages:** a high-resistance meter can read a few volts on a wire that is not connected to anything, because of voltage induced from neighbouring wires. To tell a real voltage from a ghost, load the circuit by connecting a small lamp, or measure with a low-impedance function.","**Auto-ranging and manual ranging:** an auto-ranging meter picks the range for you, and a manual one needs to be set. If the display shows OL or a rounded zero, change the range.","**Min/Max and hold:** some meters record the highest and lowest readings, which helps to catch an intermittent fault while you drive.","**Resolution and accuracy:** a 3.5 digit meter shows readings up to 1999. A good meter has an accuracy of around 0.5%. Always check the manual of the meter for its limits."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Test","Set the meter to","Connect","Healthy reading"],"rows":[["Battery at rest","DC volts","Across the battery terminals","12.4 to 12.8 V"],["Charging system","DC volts","Across the battery, engine at 2,000 rpm","13.8 to 14.7 V"],["Bulb","Ohms, bulb removed","Across the bulb terminals","A few ohms, not OL"],["Fuse","Continuity or volts","Across the fuse, circuit on, or across a fuse out of circuit","0 V across a good fuse in a live circuit"],["Wire","Continuity","Between the two ends","Near 0 Ω"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never connect the meter in current mode across the battery. This is a short circuit and can burn out the meter, melt the leads and cause injury."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always check the meter on a known good source first, such as the battery, so you can trust the readings."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l7", "title": "Voltage-drop testing", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Voltage-drop testing is the most useful method for finding high-resistance faults. It is a test that is made while the circuit is working, so it shows problems that a resistance test misses."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"voltage-drop-test"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why it works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A good conductor, such as a wire, connector or switch, drops almost no voltage when current flows through it. If the resistance is high because of corrosion, a loose terminal or a damaged strand, a measurable voltage appears across it. Measuring with a meter across the part, with the circuit operating, shows this drop directly. An ohmmeter may read a low resistance for a corroded connection with no load, but the connection can fail under load."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How to test"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch the circuit on so that the normal current is flowing, for example by turning on the headlamps.","Set the meter to a low DC volts range.","Connect the meter across the section you want to test, such as from the battery positive terminal to the load's power terminal, with the circuit running.","Read the drop. A good wire, connector or switch drops very little.","Repeat for the earth side, from the load's earth terminal to the battery negative terminal."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Acceptable drops"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Typical guide values are under 0.1 to 0.2 V across a single connection or switch, and under about 0.5 V for the whole feed or return of a circuit. Many makers give their own limits, so check the manual. A bigger drop shows resistance that needs to be found and repaired."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Finding a bad earth","steps":["A headlamp is dim. Battery voltage is 12.5 V.","Measured across the lamp, the voltage is only 10.8 V.","Measure the earth side from the lamp's earth point to the battery negative. It reads 1.4 V.","A drop of 1.4 V on the earth side shows a high-resistance earth. Clean and tighten the earth connection and test again."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Voltage-drop limits"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part tested","Typical limit"],"rows":[["A connector or splice","Under 0.1 V"],["A switch or relay contacts","Under 0.2 V"],["The whole power feed or earth return of a circuit","Under 0.5 V"],["Battery positive cable to starter, cranking","Under 0.5 V"],["Starter earth cable, cranking","Under 0.3 V"],["Alternator output cable to battery","Under 0.5 V"]],"caption":"Where a maker's figure exists, use it instead of these general guides."},
      {"t":"example","title":"A slow cranking engine","steps":["The battery reads 12.4 V at rest. The starter cranks slowly. Battery voltage during cranking is 9.8 V, which is normal for a good battery under load.","Measure from the battery positive post to the starter feed terminal while cranking. It reads 0.2 V, which is within the limit.","Measure from the engine block to the battery negative post while cranking. It reads 1.1 V, which is far above the 0.3 V limit. The earth strap is corroded.","Clean the strap and the contact surfaces, refit it tightly, and measure again. The drop falls to 0.1 V, and the starter turns faster."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Voltage-drop testing also helps with parts you cannot easily measure. A wire, a switch or a relay with a high drop wastes power as heat. If a connector feels warm after the load has been on, there is probably a high-resistance joint. Find it by feel, with a thermal camera, or with the voltage drop test."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The voltage lost in a bad connection is not available to the load. If the drop is large, the load gets less voltage than it needs."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Work along the circuit in sections. The section that shows the big drop contains the fault."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l8", "title": "Using an oscilloscope for electrical diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A multimeter shows a number. An oscilloscope shows how a voltage changes over time, which is a picture of what is happening in a circuit. Many electrical faults, such as a weak sensor signal, a flicker or an intermittent connection, can only be seen with a scope. This lesson explains the controls and shows how to read simple patterns."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"scope-signals"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading the screen"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The screen is a graph. Time runs from left to right, and voltage rises up the vertical axis. The screen is divided into squares called **divisions**. The **volts per division** control sets how many volts each vertical square represents. The **time per division** control sets how long each horizontal square lasts. Setting these well shows the signal clearly: for a 12 V signal, set 5 V per division, and for a 5 V sensor, 1 or 2 V per division."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Control","What it does","Typical setting for a crankshaft sensor"],"rows":[["Volts per division","Scales the vertical axis","2 to 5 V per division"],["Time per division","Scales the horizontal axis","5 to 20 ms per division"],["Trigger level and slope","Decides where the trace starts so the picture is stable","Just above the base line, rising edge"],["Coupling","DC shows the whole voltage. AC shows only the changes.","DC for most automotive work"],["Probe attenuation","x1 shows the actual signal. x10 divides it by ten.","x1 or x10 as set on the probe and the scope"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Connecting"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Connect the scope's earth lead to a clean earth or the battery negative, never to a signal wire.","Use a back-probe pin or a piercing probe sparingly, and seal any hole you make afterwards.","Use a high-voltage attenuator for ignition secondary signals, never connect a normal probe directly.","Keep probe leads away from ignition leads and moving parts."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Patterns you will meet"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Signal","What it looks like","What a fault looks like"],"rows":[["Battery during cranking","A line that drops to about 10 V and recovers","A deep drop below 9.6 V, or a sloping, sagging voltage"],["Alternator ripple","A nearly flat line with tiny ripple, under about 0.5 V peak to peak","Large, regular dips, which show a failed diode or winding"],["Throttle sensor sweep","A smooth rise from 0.5 to 4.5 V","Spikes, flat spots or dropouts from a worn track"],["Injector (earth-switched)","12 V that drops to near 0 V for the pulse width, and a spike when it closes","No pulse, a very short pulse, or no spike"],["PWM actuator","A square wave whose on-time changes with the duty cycle","A flat line, or a wrong frequency"],["Inductive crank sensor","An AC wave with a gap where teeth are missing","A missing wave, a very small amplitude, or noise"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Capturing an intermittent fault"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Connect the scope at the point you want to watch, and set the time per division so that you can see several seconds of signal, or use the scope's slow roll or recording mode.","Wiggle the wiring, tap the sensor or drive the vehicle with the scope recording, with an assistant if you can.","When the fault happens, stop the capture. Look back through the recording for the glitch.","Move the probe along the wiring towards the source until the glitch disappears. That marks the faulty section."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading a PWM duty cycle","steps":["A scope shows a square wave with a period of 10 ms (100 Hz). The wave is high for 3 ms and low for 7 ms.","Duty cycle = on-time ÷ period = 3 ÷ 10 = 30%.","If the ECU requests more, the high time grows, say to 6 ms, which is 60%. A flat wave that does not respond to commands suggests a problem in the driver, wiring or actuator."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Compare with a known good signal. A good waveform from a working vehicle, or from the manual, is the best reference you can have."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never connect a scope probe or ground directly to the ignition secondary or to mains. The voltages can kill and can destroy the instrument."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l9", "title": "Electrical fault finding", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Good electrical fault finding follows a method. This lesson gives you the method and then covers how to repair wires properly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The method"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Ask the customer what happens, and when. Does the fault come and go?","Check the obvious: fuse, bulb, battery voltage and connectors.","Find the circuit on the wiring diagram and decide how it should work.","Test to see which part of the circuit is working and which is not.","Repair the fault, then test the system to be sure the fault has gone."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The half-split method"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Pick a point in the middle of the circuit and test it. If the voltage is correct there, the fault is further along. If it is not, the fault is before that point. Then split the faulty half, and repeat. This finds the fault in a few tests, even in a long circuit."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical faults and tests"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","How to find it"],"rows":[["Open circuit","Voltage present up to the break and absent after it"],["Short to earth","Fuse blows. Check resistance to earth with the fuse out and the circuit disconnected"],["High resistance","Voltage drop test across sections"],["Intermittent","Wiggle the wires and connectors while monitoring, and check for water or damage"]],"caption":"Finding faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Using a jumper wire"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A jumper wire bypasses a section of a circuit to test whether it is faulty. Always use a **fused** jumper wire, and use it only to bypass a switch, relay or wire, never to connect across a load to power. Do not use it on ECU circuits unless the manual says so."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Repairing wiring"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Cut out the damaged section. Do not leave burnt or corroded wire.","Use wire of the same size or larger and the same type.","Join with a proper crimp connector or solder the joint. Crimp with the right tool.","Seal the joint with **adhesive-lined heat-shrink tubing**, which stops water getting in.","Stagger repairs on neighbouring wires so they do not make a bulky lump, and tape them back into the loom.","Support the wire so that it does not flex at the repair."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Finding a short circuit"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Remove the blown fuse and note its rating. Find the circuit on the diagram and see which loads are connected.","Disconnect the loads on the circuit one at a time, as far as you can, starting with the ones closest to the fuse box.","Connect a test lamp or a meter in place of the fuse. If the lamp glows brightly, there is still a short on the circuit. Disconnect parts until the lamp goes out. The last part removed, or the wire to it, is the fault.","Alternatively, use a **short finder**, a tester that sends a pulsing current and a hand-held detector that follows the magnetic field along the wire to the short.","Repair the fault, fit a new fuse of the correct rating, and test the circuit."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wiring repair choices"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Method","Good for","Notes"],"rows":[["Crimp butt connector","Joining wires in a loom","Use the right tool and size. Pull-test it. Seal with heat-shrink."],["Soldered joint","Small and medium wires","Heat the wire, not the solder. Use rosin-core solder. Support the joint so it does not flex."],["Sealed (weatherproof) connector","Joints outside the cabin","Water cannot enter. Good for lamps, sensors and the underside."],["Twisted wire and tape","Nothing","Never use. It corrodes and comes loose."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Match the colour and size of the wire, or label it clearly. Use the correct terminal for the connector, and a new weatherproof seal where the old one is damaged. Check the repair with a voltage drop test under load before you close up the loom."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Disconnect the battery before repairing wiring. A short circuit in a repair can cause a fire."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A poor repair becomes the next fault. Water and vibration find every weak joint. Do it properly the first time."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m09-l10", "title": "Protecting electronics and working safely on live systems", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Vehicles are full of electronic modules that are easy to damage, and expensive to replace. Many of the mistakes that damage them happen in the workshop: a reversed battery, a welding arc, a probe in the wrong place or static from your hands. This lesson explains the common causes and the habits that prevent them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Battery connection and polarity"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Always disconnect the negative terminal first and reconnect it last. This stops a spanner from shorting the positive to the body.","Never reverse the battery connections. Reverse polarity can destroy diodes in the alternator, modules and radios in a fraction of a second.","Switch everything off before you connect or disconnect the battery. Disconnecting a battery with the engine running can cause a voltage spike that damages electronics.","Never use a battery charger on a battery that is connected to the vehicle without the right procedure. Some chargers produce a voltage high enough to harm modules. Disconnect the battery or use a smart charger as the maker advises."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Static electricity (ESD)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"You can build up thousands of volts of static electricity by walking on a carpet or sliding on a seat. You do not feel it below about 3,000 V, but a chip can be damaged by a tenth of that. Touch the vehicle body before you handle a module, and hold it by its case, not by the connector pins. Keep spare modules and sensors in their anti-static bags until the moment they are fitted, and do not wear woollen or synthetic clothes that generate static near bare circuit boards."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Memory savers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **memory saver** is a small battery or power pack that plugs into the lighter socket or the diagnostic socket to keep power to the vehicle when the battery is off, so that settings such as radio codes are not lost. It is useful but has risks. It keeps all circuits live, including the airbag circuits. Never use a memory saver when you work on an airbag system, and never leave one connected during a welding job. Follow the manufacturer's instructions."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Welding and heavy equipment"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Disconnect the battery and, where the manual says, the ECU connectors, before you arc weld on a vehicle.","Place the welder's earth clamp close to the weld, so current does not travel through the vehicle's electronics.","Avoid welding near airbags, sensors or the battery.","Do not use a fast charger or a jump start pack on a vehicle with a flat or disconnected battery without the right procedure, as the voltage may spike."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"After disconnecting the battery"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the battery has been disconnected, the vehicle forgets some information. You may need to enter radio codes, reset the clock, initialise the power windows and sunroof (one-touch operation), reset the steering angle sensor, reset the service interval, and let the engine control unit relearn idle and fuel trims. The engine may idle roughly for a short time and the throttle may need a relearn. Warn the customer, and follow the procedure for each system in the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Safe testing habits"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Do","Do not"],"rows":[["Use a high-impedance multimeter on electronic circuits","Use a test lamp with a normal bulb on ECU or airbag circuits"],["Back-probe connectors from the rear","Push a probe or pin into the front of a terminal"],["Switch the ignition off before you connect or disconnect a module","Unplug a module with the ignition on"],["Use a fused jumper wire","Use an unfused wire, or bridge across a load"],["Check a wiring diagram before you cut or probe","Pierce a wire without sealing the hole afterwards"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Treat every module as if it were fragile and costly. A moment of care costs nothing, and a damaged control unit costs the customer a lot."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never work on SRS (airbag) or high-voltage systems with the power connected. Follow the manual's disconnection and waiting procedure."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Build a relay lamp circuit and find faults by voltage drop","objective":"Build and test a simple lamp circuit with a fuse, switch and relay on a board, then find an inserted open circuit and a high-resistance fault using voltage-drop testing.","time":"90 minutes","tools":["A 12 V circuit training board or battery, a fuse holder with fuses, a relay and base","A lamp or bulb, a switch and wire of the correct size","A digital multimeter and a fused jumper wire","Crimp tool, wire strippers and connectors","Wiring diagram for the training circuit"],"safety":["Disconnect the power supply while you build or change the circuit.","Use the correct fuse and never bypass it.","Do not measure resistance on a circuit with the power on.","Never place a metal tool across the battery or the supply terminals.","Keep wires clear of any hot parts and check insulation before connecting power."],"steps":["Draw the circuit: battery, fuse, relay contacts, lamp and earth, with the switch controlling the relay coil.","Connect the relay coil circuit with the switch and fuse, and connect the load circuit through the relay contacts.","Check all connections visually, then switch on the supply.","Operate the switch and confirm that the relay clicks and the lamp lights.","Measure the supply voltage, and the voltage at the lamp with the lamp on.","Ask your instructor to insert a break in the circuit. Use the half-split method to find it.","Record each test point and its voltage, and note where the voltage disappears.","Ask your instructor to insert a high-resistance fault, such as a loose connection.","Measure the voltage drop across each section of the power side with the lamp on.","Measure the voltage drop on the earth side.","Identify the section with the large voltage drop and explain what is wrong.","Repair or reset the faults and confirm the circuit works correctly."],"checks":["I built the relay circuit correctly and it worked.","I used the half-split method to find an open circuit.","I measured voltage drop across sections while the circuit was operating.","I explained the difference between an open circuit and a high-resistance fault.","I used the meter and the fused jumper wire safely."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m09-q01","q":"A lamp has a resistance of 4 Ω and is connected to a 12 V supply. What current flows?","options":["3 A","0.33 A","8 A","48 A"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l1","explain":"I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A. Dividing by the resistance gives the current."},
    {"id":"m09-q02","q":"A 60 W bulb runs on 12 V. Approximately what current does it draw?","options":["72 A","5 A","0.2 A","720 A"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l1","explain":"I = P ÷ V = 60 ÷ 12 = 5 A. Knowing the current helps you choose the fuse and the wire."},
    {"id":"m09-q03","q":"What is the purpose of the vehicle body as part of the electrical system?","options":["It acts as the fuse for every one of the circuits on the vehicle","It raises the voltage at the lights and the motors when the engine runs","It stores the electrical charge that the starter motor needs on a cold morning","It provides the return path for current back to the battery negative"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l1","explain":"Using the body as an earth return saves wire, but a poor earth causes faults."},
    {"id":"m09-q04","q":"A 12 V circuit has a total resistance of 4 Ω. What current flows?","options":["48 A","0.33 A","3 A","8 A"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l1","explain":"Current = voltage ÷ resistance = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A. Multiplying would give 48, which is the wrong operation."},
    {"id":"m09-q05","q":"Which prefix means one thousandth?","options":["Milli","Kilo","Mega","Micro"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l1","explain":"Milli means 1/1,000, so 250 mA is 0.25 A. Kilo is a thousand times, mega a million times, and micro a millionth."},
    {"id":"m09-q06","q":"What happens to the current in a series circuit when one component breaks?","options":["The current stops flowing in the whole circuit","The current flows through the vehicle body instead of through the wires","Only the broken component stops and the rest carry on working as before","The current in all of the other components in the circuit rises at once"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"A series circuit has only one path. Any break stops all current."},
    {"id":"m09-q07","q":"In a parallel circuit, what is the voltage across each branch?","options":["The same, equal to the supply voltage","It is shared in proportion to the resistance of the branches","It is zero in the branch with the lowest resistance","It is higher in the branch with the most resistance"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"Each branch is connected across the same two points, so it sees the same voltage."},
    {"id":"m09-q08","q":"A dim headlamp has 12.5 V at the battery but only 10.8 V at the lamp. What is the likely cause?","options":["The fuse is rated too high for the circuit","High resistance in a connection or earth, dropping voltage","The lamp is brighter than normal and has a lower resistance","The battery is fully charged and needs less current"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"Voltage is lost across a high-resistance part of the circuit and the lamp gets less."},
    {"id":"m09-q09","q":"What does a short to earth usually cause?","options":["A battery that charges faster","Excess current that blows the fuse","A circuit that works but is dim","A circuit that works at higher voltage"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"A short bypasses the load, so the current rises and the fuse melts."},
    {"id":"m09-q10","q":"In a parallel circuit, one lamp blows. What happens to the others?","options":["They get brighter, because the current that the lamp used now goes to them","They keep working, because each has its own path","They get dimmer, because the supply voltage is now shared between them","They go out too, because the current stops flowing in every branch of the circuit"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"Each branch is connected across the supply and has its own path, so a break in one branch does not affect the others."},
    {"id":"m09-q11","q":"A 12 V circuit has two 6 Ω resistors in series. What is the current?","options":["2 A","0.5 A","4 A","1 A"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"Total resistance is 12 Ω, and I = 12 ÷ 12 = 1 A."},
    {"id":"m09-q12","q":"What is the function of a resistor in a voltage divider?","options":["To open the circuit when the sensor becomes hotter than its normal limit","To allow the current to flow in both directions through the sensor","To store charge for later use and to smooth out the voltage in the circuit","To set a fraction of the supply voltage at the point between two resistors"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"Sensors such as temperature sensors use dividers, and the ECU reads the middle voltage."},
    {"id":"m09-q13","q":"A wire is too thin for the load current. What is the risk?","options":["The load will run faster than normal and draw more current from the supply","It can overheat and set fire to the insulation","The fuse will not blow, so nothing in the circuit will ever be protected","The voltage will rise above the battery voltage along the length of wire"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l2","explain":"Use a wire of the correct size for the current."},
    {"id":"m09-q14","q":"Two 3 Ω lamps are wired in parallel across 12 V. What is the total current?","options":["4 A","8 A","2 A","12 A"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l3","explain":"Each lamp draws 12 ÷ 3 = 4 A and the currents add in a parallel circuit, so the total is 8 A."},
    {"id":"m09-q15","q":"A 4 Ω and a 2 Ω resistor are in series across 12 V. What is the voltage across the 4 Ω resistor?","options":["4 V","6 V","12 V","8 V"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l3","explain":"The current is 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A. Voltage across the 4 Ω resistor is 2 × 4 = 8 V and across the 2 Ω it is 4 V."},
    {"id":"m09-q16","q":"A wire has a resistance of 0.115 Ω and carries 5 A. What is the voltage drop?","options":["About 0.58 V","About 43 V","About 0.023 V","About 5.1 V"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l3","explain":"Voltage drop = current × resistance = 5 × 0.115 = 0.575 V. It is above the usual 0.5 V limit for a whole feed."},
    {"id":"m09-q17","q":"A 55 W headlamp runs on 12 V. About what current does it draw?","options":["About 660 A","About 4.6 A","About 0.2 A","About 43 A"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l3","explain":"Current = power ÷ voltage = 55 ÷ 12 = 4.6 A."},
    {"id":"m09-q18","q":"A load draws 5 A on a 1.0 mm² wire that is safe to 10 A. Which fuse is most suitable?","options":["A 25 A fuse, for extra margin","A 2 A fuse, to keep it safe","A 30 A fuse, as for a motor","A 7.5 A or 10 A fuse"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l3","explain":"The fuse should be above the load but at or below what the wire can carry. A 25 A fuse would let the wire overheat before it blew."},
    {"id":"m09-q19","q":"Why must a blown fuse be replaced with one of the same rating?","options":["Different fuses have different colours and these colours must match the wire","A smaller fuse would not fit in the holder, and the circuit would not work","A larger fuse makes the circuit work better and prevents false blowing","A larger fuse allows the wire to overheat before it blows"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l4","explain":"The fuse protects the wire. A higher rating would let a damaged circuit burn."},
    {"id":"m09-q20","q":"How does a relay work?","options":["A large current in a coil closes contacts that carry a much smaller current","A switch changes the polarity of the supply that goes to the load","A small current in a coil closes contacts that carry a larger current","A thermal strip bends and opens the circuit when it becomes too hot"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l4","explain":"The small coil current is controlled by a switch or ECU, and the contacts handle the heavy load."},
    {"id":"m09-q21","q":"Which relay terminals connect to the coil?","options":["30 and 85","87 and 87a","85 and 86","30 and 87"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l4","explain":"30 and 87 are the switch contacts. 87a is the normally closed contact."},
    {"id":"m09-q22","q":"What does a diode do in a circuit?","options":["It limits the current in the circuit by adding resistance to the path","It opens the circuit when the temperature of the wire rises too far","It stores a small charge and smooths out the voltage in the circuit","It allows current to flow in one direction only"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l4","explain":"Diodes are used in alternators to turn AC into DC."},
    {"id":"m09-q23","q":"A circuit breaker differs from a fuse because it does what?","options":["It cannot protect against a short circuit, only against a slow overload","It operates at much lower currents than a fuse and so protects small wires","It can be reset after it trips, while a fuse must be replaced","It is used only in the ignition circuit and nowhere else in the vehicle"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l4","explain":"Some circuits, such as power windows, use resettable breakers."},
    {"id":"m09-q24","q":"What fuse rating is a red blade fuse?","options":["5 A","15 A","10 A","30 A"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l4","explain":"Standard colours: tan 5 A, brown 7.5 A, red 10 A, blue 15 A, yellow 20 A, white 25 A, green 30 A."},
    {"id":"m09-q25","q":"What usually happens when a transistor in a control unit fails?","options":["It repairs itself after cooling down","It sticks on or off","It makes the load run half as fast","It turns into a fuse for the circuit"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l4","explain":"A failed switch transistor is typically stuck on or stuck off. The load runs all the time or never runs."},
    {"id":"m09-q26","q":"What does a relay allow a small current to do?","options":["Store a large amount of charge","Convert AC into DC","Switch a much larger current","Measure the battery voltage"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l5","explain":"A small current in the coil makes an electromagnet that closes heavy contacts, so thin wires and a small switch can control a big load."},
    {"id":"m09-q27","q":"Which relay terminals are normally the coil?","options":["30 and 87","87 and 87a","30 and 85","85 and 86"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l5","explain":"The coil is 85 and 86. The switched contacts are 30 (common) and 87 (normally open) or 87a (normally closed)."},
    {"id":"m09-q28","q":"Why does a relay with a suppression diode need the correct polarity?","options":["The diode shorts the coil if reversed","The diode only works on alternating current","The coil would burn at once if it were reversed","The contacts would join the wrong terminals"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l5","explain":"The diode is across the coil to absorb the voltage spike. If you apply supply the wrong way round, the diode becomes a short circuit across the coil."},
    {"id":"m09-q29","q":"Why does a stalled motor draw a very high current?","options":["It makes the battery produce more voltage","Its brushes become much thicker","Its fuse is faulty in some way","No opposing voltage when it is stalled"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l5","explain":"A turning motor induces a voltage that opposes the supply and limits the current. When stalled, nothing limits it but the winding resistance."},
    {"id":"m09-q30","q":"A relay coil measures 90 Ω across terminals 85 and 86. What does this suggest?","options":["The coil is probably fine","The coil has an open circuit","The contacts are burnt","The relay is rated for 24 V only"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l5","explain":"A coil resistance of 60 to 120 Ω is typical. OL would mean an open coil, and a very low value would mean a short."},
    {"id":"m09-q31","q":"How should a multimeter be connected to measure voltage across a lamp?","options":["In series, in the wire leading to the lamp","Across the battery only, never across a lamp","In parallel, across the lamp terminals","In series, with the lamp switched off"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l6","explain":"Voltage is measured across a component. Current is measured in series."},
    {"id":"m09-q32","q":"When can you measure resistance in a circuit?","options":["While the load is working at full current and the meter is clamped","With the power on and the engine running at a steady fast idle","Only when the power is off and the component is disconnected","Only when the battery is fully charged and the engine is warm"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l6","explain":"A live circuit gives a wrong reading and can damage the meter."},
    {"id":"m09-q33","q":"Why is a test lamp unsuitable for testing ECU circuits?","options":["It is only suitable for testing the battery terminals and the starter","It draws too much current and can damage electronic circuits","It will not work on a negative earth system and so is not reliable","It does not light with a 12 V supply and gives a false reading on any circuit"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l6","explain":"Use a digital multimeter on electronic circuits."},
    {"id":"m09-q34","q":"What is back-probing?","options":["Testing a connector from the rear while it is plugged in, using a fine probe","Testing the battery terminals through the cable insulation without removing the clamp","Testing the circuit by disconnecting the earth strap before every reading","Testing a connector from the front by pushing the probe into the terminal opening"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l6","explain":"Back-probing avoids damaging the terminal contacts and the seals."},
    {"id":"m09-q35","q":"Why must a meter never be connected across the battery in current mode?","options":["The meter will show the wrong voltage and the reading will be useless","It makes a short circuit that can burn out the meter and cause injury","The battery will go flat in a few seconds and will never recover","The battery will charge too fast and the cells will be damaged by gas"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l6","explain":"Current mode has almost no resistance, so a huge current can flow."},
    {"id":"m09-q36","q":"What does a voltage reading of 12.6 V at rest on a battery suggest?","options":["A short circuit somewhere in the starting system","A faulty alternator that is overcharging the battery","A fully charged battery","A completely flat battery that will not start the engine"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l6","explain":"A rested, fully charged lead-acid battery reads about 12.6 V."},
    {"id":"m09-q37","q":"When is a voltage-drop test carried out?","options":["While the circuit is working and current is flowing","With the circuit switched off and the component disconnected from the wire","Only when the engine is hot and the thermostat is fully open","With the battery removed from the vehicle and the meter set to ohms"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l7","explain":"A high-resistance connection only shows a drop when current flows through it."},
    {"id":"m09-q38","q":"What is a typical acceptable voltage drop across a single connection?","options":["Less than about 0.1 to 0.2 V","About 6 V","About half of the battery voltage","About 2 V"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l7","explain":"A good connection drops very little. Check the manual for limits."},
    {"id":"m09-q39","q":"A voltage drop of 1.4 V is found on the earth side of a lamp circuit. What does this show?","options":["A faulty bulb filament that is drawing far too little current","A blown fuse in the supply side of the circuit before the lamp","A fully working earth connection that is carrying the current properly","A high-resistance earth that needs cleaning and tightening"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l7","explain":"Too much voltage is being lost in the earth path."},
    {"id":"m09-q40","q":"The earth side of a starter circuit shows 1.1 V drop during cranking. The limit is 0.3 V. What is the fault?","options":["A faulty starter solenoid contact","A high-resistance earth cable","A flat spare tyre in the boot","A blocked fuel filter on the engine"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l7","explain":"A drop above the limit on the earth side shows resistance in the earth path. Clean and tighten the strap, or replace the cable."},
    {"id":"m09-q41","q":"Which test finds a high-resistance fault in a working circuit?","options":["A static resistance test with the power off","A fuse continuity test","A bulb test with an ohmmeter","A voltage-drop test"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l7","explain":"A voltage drop test is done with the circuit loaded, so a faulty connection that only fails under current shows up."},
    {"id":"m09-q42","q":"What does the time per division setting on a scope control?","options":["How many volts each vertical square shows","The brightness of the displayed trace","How long each horizontal square lasts","The battery voltage of the scope itself"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l8","explain":"Time per division scales the horizontal axis. Volts per division scales the vertical axis."},
    {"id":"m09-q43","q":"A PWM signal is high for 3 ms of a 10 ms period. What is the duty cycle?","options":["3%","30%","70%","13%"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l8","explain":"Duty cycle = on-time ÷ period = 3 ÷ 10 = 30%."},
    {"id":"m09-q44","q":"A scope trace of alternator output shows large regular dips. What does it suggest?","options":["A failed diode or a faulty winding","A fully charged battery","A loose drive belt on the alternator","A weak starter motor on the engine"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l8","explain":"A good alternator gives a nearly flat trace with a very small ripple. Large regular dips show that one of the phases is not delivering current."},
    {"id":"m09-q45","q":"Where should the scope earth lead be connected?","options":["To the signal wire being measured","To the ignition secondary lead","To battery negative or a clean earth","To the positive terminal of the battery"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l8","explain":"The earth lead is the reference for all measurements. Connecting it to a live wire makes a short circuit."},
    {"id":"m09-q46","q":"What is the half-split method?","options":["Testing the middle of a circuit and halving the faulty section each time","Splitting a wire in half to test each part of the conductor separately","Replacing half of the parts in the circuit at once to save time","Removing half of the fuses from the circuit to see what happens"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"It finds the fault in the fewest tests, because each test rules out half of the remaining circuit instead of checking every part in turn."},
    {"id":"m09-q47","q":"Which fault is hardest to find, because the circuit still works?","options":["High resistance","A flat battery","Open circuit","Short to earth that blows the fuse"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"The load works poorly, and a continuity test may look normal."},
    {"id":"m09-q48","q":"What should a fused jumper wire be used for?","options":["To connect a load directly across the battery for a quick check of its motor","To test an airbag circuit and see whether the module will fire","To bypass a switch, relay or wire to see if it is faulty","To replace a blown fuse permanently so the customer does not notice"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"The fuse protects the wire. Never use a jumper on a load across the supply."},
    {"id":"m09-q49","q":"What is the correct way to repair a damaged wire?","options":["Join the wire with a thin piece of wire of any size and cover it","Leave the damaged section in place and tape over the bare strands","Cut out the damage, crimp or solder in new wire and seal with adhesive-lined heat-shrink","Twist the broken ends together and wrap the joint tightly with insulating tape"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"A sealed, secure joint stops water and vibration from causing another fault."},
    {"id":"m09-q50","q":"What must you do before repairing wiring?","options":["Remove the radio fuse only","Disconnect the battery","Run the engine to warm the wires","Switch on all the lights"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"A short circuit during repair can cause a fire."},
    {"id":"m09-q51","q":"An intermittent fault is best found by doing what?","options":["Replacing the load straight away and then testing whether the fault has gone","Disconnecting the battery for an hour so the circuit can reset itself","Fitting a larger fuse so that the intermittent fault no longer blows it","Wiggling wires and connectors while monitoring the circuit"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"Intermittent faults often come from loose pins and damaged wires."},
    {"id":"m09-q52","q":"A fuse blows again at once after replacement. What should you do?","options":["Fit a larger fuse so it does not blow","Find the short before fitting a fuse","Remove the fuse and bridge with a wire","Wait a day and try the circuit again"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"A fuse that blows immediately shows a short circuit. A larger fuse would let the wire overheat instead."},
    {"id":"m09-q53","q":"Which repair gives a lasting joint in a wire outside the cabin?","options":["Twisted wires wrapped in some tape","Two wires pushed under a loose screw","A knot tied with a dab of grease","A sealed crimp or soldered joint"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l9","explain":"The joint must be mechanically strong and sealed against water. Twisted and taped repairs corrode and fail."},
    {"id":"m09-q54","q":"Which battery terminal should be disconnected first?","options":["The positive terminal","Both together at the same time","The negative terminal","The terminal that is cleaner"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m09-l10","explain":"With the negative off, the body is no longer a return path, so a spanner on the positive cannot cause a short to the body."},
    {"id":"m09-q55","q":"Why should you touch the body of the vehicle before handling a module?","options":["To discharge static from your body","To prove the vehicle is earthed properly","To cool the fingers before touching","To warm the module to the same temperature"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l10","explain":"Static charge can be thousands of volts, and sensitive chips can be damaged by a fraction of that without you feeling it."},
    {"id":"m09-q56","q":"Why should a memory saver never be used when working on an airbag system?","options":["It makes the airbag light turn red","It would erase the airbag's fault codes","It keeps airbag circuits live","It forces the airbag to deploy at once"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m09-l10","explain":"With the saver connected the supply is never isolated, so the airbag circuits remain live and could fire if disturbed."},
    {"id":"m09-q57","q":"What is a common effect of reversing the battery connections?","options":["Diodes and modules can be destroyed","The battery becomes fully charged","The engine runs more smoothly","Nothing happens as the system is protected"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l10","explain":"Reverse polarity can destroy diodes and electronics in a fraction of a second. Fuses and protection do not always help."},
    {"id":"m09-q58","q":"After reconnecting a battery, what may the vehicle need?","options":["A new set of spark plugs, for sure","A change of the engine oil as well","A replacement air filter in the box","Relearns, e.g. idle and windows"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m09-l10","explain":"Disconnecting the battery clears learned values. Some systems need initialising before they work normally."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m09-c01","front":"State Ohm's law.","back":"V = I × R. Voltage equals current multiplied by resistance.","lesson":"m09-l1"},
    {"id":"m09-c02","front":"State the power formula.","back":"P = V × I. Power in watts equals volts multiplied by amps.","lesson":"m09-l1"},
    {"id":"m09-c03","front":"What is the vehicle earth (ground)?","back":"The body and chassis used as the return path to the battery negative.","lesson":"m09-l1"},
    {"id":"m09-c04","front":"What does 250 mA equal in amps?","back":"0.25 A. Milli means one thousandth.","lesson":"m09-l1"},
    {"id":"m09-c05","front":"What is the current in a series circuit?","back":"The same everywhere in the circuit.","lesson":"m09-l2"},
    {"id":"m09-c06","front":"What is the voltage across parallel branches?","back":"The same across each branch.","lesson":"m09-l2"},
    {"id":"m09-c07","front":"What is an open circuit?","back":"A break in the path, so no current flows.","lesson":"m09-l2"},
    {"id":"m09-c08","front":"What is a short to earth?","back":"A power wire touching the body or earth, causing excess current and a blown fuse.","lesson":"m09-l2"},
    {"id":"m09-c09","front":"What does high resistance cause?","back":"Dim lights and slow motors because voltage is lost in the fault.","lesson":"m09-l2"},
    {"id":"m09-c10","front":"What is a voltage divider?","back":"Two resistors in series that give a fraction of the supply voltage between them.","lesson":"m09-l2"},
    {"id":"m09-c11","front":"Total resistance of two resistors in series and in parallel?","back":"Series: R1 + R2. Parallel: 1 ÷ (1/R1 + 1/R2). Parallel is always less than the smallest.","lesson":"m09-l3"},
    {"id":"m09-c12","front":"Voltage divider formula?","back":"V out = V in × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2), measured across R2.","lesson":"m09-l3"},
    {"id":"m09-c13","front":"Resistance of a copper wire?","back":"R = 0.0172 × L ÷ A, with L in total metres out and back, and A in mm².","lesson":"m09-l3"},
    {"id":"m09-c14","front":"Rule for choosing a fuse?","back":"Above the normal load, by 25 to 50%, and at or below what the wire can carry.","lesson":"m09-l3"},
    {"id":"m09-c15","front":"What does a fuse protect?","back":"The wire. It melts if the current is too high.","lesson":"m09-l4"},
    {"id":"m09-c16","front":"Why not fit a larger fuse?","back":"The wire could overheat and burn before the fuse blows.","lesson":"m09-l4"},
    {"id":"m09-c17","front":"What do relay terminals 85 and 86 connect?","back":"The coil of the relay.","lesson":"m09-l4"},
    {"id":"m09-c18","front":"What do relay terminals 30 and 87 connect?","back":"The switched contacts that carry the load current.","lesson":"m09-l4"},
    {"id":"m09-c19","front":"What does a diode do?","back":"It lets current flow in one direction only.","lesson":"m09-l4"},
    {"id":"m09-c20","front":"Blade fuse colours for 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 A?","back":"Tan, red, blue, yellow, white (clear), green.","lesson":"m09-l4"},
    {"id":"m09-c21","front":"Relay terminal numbers?","back":"85 and 86 are the coil, 30 is the common, 87 is normally open and 87a is normally closed.","lesson":"m09-l5"},
    {"id":"m09-c22","front":"Why does a stalled motor draw a large current?","back":"No opposing voltage is generated when it is not turning.","lesson":"m09-l5"},
    {"id":"m09-c23","front":"What does a suppression diode do?","back":"It absorbs the voltage spike when a coil is switched off, protecting the switching transistor.","lesson":"m09-l5"},
    {"id":"m09-c24","front":"What is electromagnetic induction?","back":"A voltage is induced in a wire when the magnetic field across it changes, as in an alternator or ignition coil.","lesson":"m09-l5"},
    {"id":"m09-c25","front":"How is voltage measured?","back":"In parallel, across the component.","lesson":"m09-l6"},
    {"id":"m09-c26","front":"How is current measured?","back":"In series, with the meter in the path of the current.","lesson":"m09-l6"},
    {"id":"m09-c27","front":"When is resistance measured?","back":"Only with the power off and the component disconnected.","lesson":"m09-l6"},
    {"id":"m09-c28","front":"Why not use a test lamp on ECU circuits?","back":"It draws too much current and can damage electronics.","lesson":"m09-l6"},
    {"id":"m09-c29","front":"What is back-probing?","back":"Testing a plugged-in connector from the rear with a fine probe.","lesson":"m09-l6"},
    {"id":"m09-c30","front":"Why does a DMM read a ghost voltage?","back":"Its very high input resistance picks up voltage induced from neighbouring wires. Load the circuit to check.","lesson":"m09-l6"},
    {"id":"m09-c31","front":"When is a voltage-drop test made?","back":"While the circuit is working with current flowing.","lesson":"m09-l7"},
    {"id":"m09-c32","front":"What is a typical acceptable drop across one connection?","back":"Less than about 0.1 to 0.2 V.","lesson":"m09-l7"},
    {"id":"m09-c33","front":"What does a big drop on the earth side mean?","back":"A high-resistance earth connection.","lesson":"m09-l7"},
    {"id":"m09-c34","front":"Voltage drop limits for a connector and a switch?","back":"Under 0.1 V across a connector, under 0.2 V across a switch, and under about 0.5 V for a whole feed or earth.","lesson":"m09-l7"},
    {"id":"m09-c35","front":"What do volts per division and time per division set?","back":"Volts per division scales the vertical axis. Time per division scales the horizontal axis.","lesson":"m09-l8"},
    {"id":"m09-c36","front":"How do you find a PWM duty cycle?","back":"On-time divided by the period, as a percentage.","lesson":"m09-l8"},
    {"id":"m09-c37","front":"Healthy alternator ripple on a scope?","back":"A nearly flat line with a ripple under about 0.5 V peak to peak.","lesson":"m09-l8"},
    {"id":"m09-c38","front":"What is the half-split method?","back":"Test the middle of the circuit and keep halving the faulty part.","lesson":"m09-l9"},
    {"id":"m09-c39","front":"Why use a fused jumper wire?","back":"The fuse protects the wire and the circuit from a short.","lesson":"m09-l9"},
    {"id":"m09-c40","front":"How should you seal a wire repair?","back":"With adhesive-lined heat-shrink tubing.","lesson":"m09-l9"},
    {"id":"m09-c41","front":"What must you do before wiring repairs?","back":"Disconnect the battery.","lesson":"m09-l9"},
    {"id":"m09-c42","front":"How do you find a short circuit?","back":"Remove the fuse, disconnect loads one at a time with a lamp or meter in the fuse holder, and see when the short clears.","lesson":"m09-l9"},
    {"id":"m09-c43","front":"What should you do before handling a module?","back":"Touch the body to discharge static, hold it by the case and keep it in its anti-static bag until it is fitted.","lesson":"m09-l10"},
    {"id":"m09-c44","front":"What may need relearning after a battery disconnect?","back":"Radio codes, clock, windows, steering angle sensor, idle and fuel trims, and the service reminder.","lesson":"m09-l10"}
  ]
});
